  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:  \n   The goal is to find a non-negative sequence that minimizes the supremum-normalized self-convolution ratio.  \n   I'll use a probabilistic optimization approach combined with deterministic polishing:  \n   - Phase 1: Random restarts with exponential cooling for global minimum exploration.  \n   - Phase 2: Once a good local minimum is found, use a coordinate-wise optimizer to escape stuck regions.  \n   - Phase 3: Finalize with mirror descent polishing for improved precision.  \n   This differs from deterministic methods by incorporating randomness with Bayesian learning of search boundaries, balancing exploration/exploitation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:  \n   Representation: \\( a = [a(0), a(1), \\dots, a(n-1)] \\) with \\( n \\) fixed (512).  \n   Objective: \\( \\min \\frac{2n \\cdot \\max(\\text{convolve}(a,a,\\text{mode}=\\text{full}))}{(\\sum a)^2} \\)  \n   Constraints: \\( 0 \\leq a_i \\leq 1000 \\), \\( \\sum a \\geq 0.01 \\)  \n   Use Lagrangian relaxation with piecewise chamfer distance functions as constraints.  \n   Help functions:  \n     - `autodrop_outliers(X)` uses Otsu thresholding to mark indices as \"inactive\" (likely near zero).  \n     - `doubling_gradient_est()` computes finite-difference gradient estimates without using father's function for complex decay.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:  \n   Steps:  \n   A. Initialization:  \n        if 'parent' in kwargs: base0 = kwargs['parent'] (uniform normalized by default)  \n        else: try_len=512, initialize candidate_sequence with `make_symmetric_init(512, 'bump')`\n\n   B.Poly\u6539\u9769\u521b\u65b0 optimization:  \n        while time_left:  \n          (1) Generate random local perturbations...  \n          (2) Estimate gradient via first-order finite difference...  \n          (3) Update sequence following adversarial gradient...  \n          (4) Monitor binding constraints (whether the gradient is active or not)\n\n   C.CoordinateDescent \uc9c0\uc5ed\ud68c\uc0c9\uae30:  \n        Try small weight adjustments at indices with low values.  \n        Monitor improvement in max convolution score.\n\n   D.Finale polished by mirror descent:  \n        Use the soft-max routine to compute customized minimizer steps.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:  \n   - Phase1: Hyperparameter regime \"thermal_optimization\" with exponentially decaying temperature.  \n   - Phase2: Tracks \"active_indices\" via dropout (capping low values)  \n   - Phase3: Fine-tuning with Adam-style updates (lr=0.002, top_k=None, simplex=true)  \n   - Trigger conditions: If child finds a lower object, reel up and restart from best found.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:  \n   Total time: ~1800s allocated to:  \n     \u2022 Phase0 (initialization): 5s  \n     \u2022 Phase1 (random searches): 800s (16 perturbations)  \n     \u2022 Phase2 (refined search): 500s (10 points)  \n     \u2022 Phase3 (polishing): 500s (5 Adam steps)  \n\n   Uses numpy \u0627\u0644\u0637\u0644Reasoning\u3092\u4e0e\ud3ed\u675c\u7edd any floatingink\u6a13.  \n   Includes fallback to simplex projection if optimization diverges.\n\n6. Validation and return contract:  \n   And roll all sequences through evaluate_sequence to enforce normalization.  \n   Final sequence must have sum >=0.01 and all values in [0,1000]. Return best_founded_vectors.\n\n   This design provides an optimized solution that should find a quasi-global minimum sequence (~1.501) safely under 1800s.",
